      "dom_id": "question-90970",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "What is the range of the function $f(x)=-{x}^{2}-8x-4?$",
      "worked_solution": "The given parabola is written in standard form,\n\n$$\ny=a{x}^{2}+bx+c,\n$$\n\nwith $a=-1<0,$ $b=-8,$ and $c=-4.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the parabola opens downward. Consequently, there is no minimum output, and the vertex corresponds to the maximum output.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}=-\\frac{(-8)}{2\\cdot (-1)}=-4.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=-4$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{x}^{2}-8x-4 \\\\  & =-(-4)^{2}-8(-4)-4 \\\\  & =-16+32-4 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(-4,12),$ and the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,12].$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14243803/assets/52d836e66719861c2fff226f5ae8d38485d3874b51365dddf8e3d3e99088972c.png)",
