      "dom_id": "question-250613",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the range of the function $f(x)=-(x+6)^{2}-10.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$<f(x)$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}",
      "worked_solution": "The parabola is written in vertex form, \n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^{2}+k,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the leading coefficient and $(h,k)$ is the vertex. \n\n- Since $h=-6$ and $k=-10,$ the vertex is $(-6,-10).$\n\n- Since $a=-1<0,$ the parabola opens downwards.\n\nBecause the parabola opens downwards, there is no minimum output, and the vertex corresponds to the maximum output.\n\nTherefore, the range is $(-\\infty ,-10],$ i.e. $-\\infty <f(x)\\le -10.$\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14243803/assets/7aa50770fd1c24866ca5d5a8df1b04dc7260b4a5b346f8296757c59d33ba545b.png)",
